harmless:
And every morning brings such pleasure
Of sweet love-making, harmless sport:
Love, that makes and finds its treasure;

harriet:
Saw none he despised, though poor and black;
And Harriet Beecher, whose glowing pen
Corroded the chains of fettered men.

harp:
With heaving breast and eyes aflame;
The Harp he took all undismayed,
Smote on its strings, still strange to song,

She sang as she worked,
And the harp-strings spoke;
Her voice never faltered,

harbinger:
Lovers' oaths are thin as rain;
Love's a harbinger of pain--
Would it were not so!

harmoniously:
To live mine straight out, vocally, in books;
Harmoniously for others, if indeed
A woman’s soul, like man’s, be wide enough

harshly:
Like a gnarled and stunted tree,
Bearing harshly of my youth
Puckered fruit that sears the mouth;

What should have been an inner instinct’s feat;
Or as a prose-wit, harshly poet turned,
Lacking the subtler music in his measure,

harms:
Impregnate with old Chinese charms;
Sealed urns containing mortal harms,
They fill the mind with thoughts impure,

His baby to her heart:
'Even let it go, the love that harms:
We twain will never part;

harked:
I paused at every grievous door,
And harked a moment, holding up my hand,--and for a space
A hush was on them, while they watched my face;

harpist:
The rain streams down like harp-strings from the sky;
The wind, that world-old harpist sitteth by;
And ever as he sings his low refrain,

hart's:
